@node Type utilities
@section Type utilities

Provided by the module @code{(hnh util type)}

@subsection Type Clauses
@anchor{type-clause}
@cindex type-clause

Type clauses are an effective way of writing compound predicates
without explicitly mentioning the variable at all steps.

The simplest type predicate is a single symbol, which is directly
called on the object:
@example
predicate? ⇒ (predicate? x)
@end example

Otherwise, if the predicate is a list then the variable is spliced
into the argument list in the first position:
@example
(proc args ...) ⇒ (proc x args ...)
@end example

The two primitives @code{and} and @code{or} are also available, which
both take an arbitrary number of predicates, and calls them in order,
with Scheme's usual short-circuiting rules.
@footnote{@code{and} and @code{or} doesn't have to be primitives, but
we would otherwise have one hell of a namespace conflict}

@defmac list-of variable type-clause
Checks if @var{variable} is a list, and that every element satisfies type-clause.
@end defmac

@defmac non-empty-list-of variable type-clause
Alias for @code{(pair-of @var{type-clause} (list-of @var{type-clause}))}.
@end defmac

@defmac pair-of variable car-type-clause cdr-type-clause
Check if @var{variable} is a cons-pair, and that the car satisfies
@var{car-type-clause}, and that the cdr satisfies @var{cdr-type-clause}.
@end defmac

@defmac pair-of* variable car-type-clause rest-type-clauses ...
Like @code{cons*} is to @code{cons}, this is to @code{pair-of}.

Note that for proper lists the last element must be @code{(listof @var{predicate?})}
@end defmac

@defmac tuple-of variable field-type-clauses ...
Tuples here are defined as lists of exact length, where each index is
assigned its own type.

For example, @code{(tuple-of string? number? number?)} would match a
list of length 3, starting with one string followed by two numbers.
@end defmac

@subsection Definitions

@defmac build-validator-body variable type-clause
``Entry point'' of type clauses. Inserts variable into the
type-clause, returning something ready to be passed along the eval (or
rather, spliced into another macro).

Also used if new ``primitives'' are to be added, such as list-of.
@end defmac

@defmac typecheck variable type-clause [procedure-name=(current-procedure-name)] [type-source=(quote type-clause)]
Checks @var{variable} against @var{type-clause}, and raises
@code{'wrong-type-arg} if it fails. @var{procedure-name} is used
as the calling procedure for @code{scm-error}.

Useful at the start of procedures.
@end defmac

@deftp {Error} wrong-type-arg procedure fmt args #f
@end deftp

@defmac current-procedure-name
Returns the current procedure name as a symbol, or @code{#f} if not found.
@end defmac


@defun false? x
An alias of @code{not}. Usefull in type predicates when introducing optional
types, since
@example
(or false? integer?)
@end example
is much clearer than
@example
(or not integer?)
@end example
@end defun


@defun any-type
Convenience type, accepting any value.
@end defun


@subsection Named Types

Named types are provided by the module @code{(hnh util named-type)},
to avoid loops in the dependency graph.

@defmac named-type type
Due to how @code{typecheck} works, the fancy type combinators can only
be used without explicitly passing along a variable when deployed
within the @code{typecheck} form@footnote{@code{(hnh util object)}
gets around this by just expanding into @code{typecheck} forms}.

Sometimes you however need higher ordered types, the following works,
but reports @code{my-type} as the invalid type, which is less than
helpful.
@example
(define my-type (lambda (x) (list-of x string?)))
(typecheck '("") my-type)
@end example

Instead, named types exists, which does the macro expansion usually
done in @code{typecheck} ahead of time, and gives a record which can
later be passed to typecheck (double parenthesis due to how
typecheck expands), which does exactly what the inline version would
have done:

@example
(define my-type (named-type (list-of string?))
(typecheck '("")
           ((named-type-type my-type))
           (current-procedure-name)
           (named-type-name my-type))
@end example
@end defmac

@defun named-type? x
@end defun

@defun named-type-name named-type
Returns the source expression, quoted.
@end defun

@defun named-type-type named-type
Returns a procedure matching the declared type
@end defun
